# LLM Integration for Spell Generation

This file defines rules and patterns for integrating with Large Language Models (LLMs) in Spellwright. The LLM acts as a "compiler" that translates natural language into structured JSON.

## Complete Data Flow

```
1. User Input (Natural Language)
   ↓
2. Unity Client (Prompt Construction)
   ↓
3. HTTPS Request to LLM API
   ↓
4. LLM API Service (Processing)
   ↓
5. HTTPS Response (Structured JSON)
   ↓
6. JSON Validator (Unity Client)
   ↓
7. ECS Baking System (Entity Creation)
   ↓
8. Runtime Systems (Execution)
```

## Rule 1: Mandatory Asynchronous Communication

### NEVER Block the Main Thread

All communication with the LLM API MUST be asynchronous using `async/await`.

### Implementation Pattern

```csharp
using UnityEngine.Networking;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class LLMSpellCreationService
{
    private const string API_ENDPOINT = "https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions";
    private string apiKey;

    public async Task<string> CreateSpellJsonAsync(string userPrompt)
    {
        // 1. Build request payload
        var requestPayload = BuildRequestPayload(userPrompt);

        // 2. Create HTTP request
        using UnityWebRequest request = new UnityWebRequest(API_ENDPOINT, "POST");
        byte[] bodyRaw = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(requestPayload);
        request.uploadHandler = new UploadHandlerRaw(bodyRaw);
        request.downloadHandler = new DownloadHandlerBuffer();

        // 3. Headers
        request.SetRequestHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
        request.SetRequestHeader("Authorization", $"Bearer {apiKey}");

        // 4. Send asynchronous request
        var operation = request.SendWebRequest();

        // 5. Wait for response without blocking
        while (!operation.isDone)
        {
            await Task.Yield();
        }

        // 6. Check for errors
        if (request.result != UnityWebRequest.Result.Success)
        {
            throw new System.Exception($"LLM API Error: {request.error}");
        }

        // 7. Extract JSON from response
        string responseText = request.downloadHandler.text;
        string spellJson = ExtractJsonFromResponse(responseText);

        return spellJson;
    }
}
```

### Client Usage

```csharp
public class SpellCreationUI : MonoBehaviour
{
    private LLMSpellCreationService llmService;

    public async void OnCreateSpellButtonClicked()
    {
        string userInput = inputField.text;

        try
        {
            // Doesn't block UI
            string spellJson = await llmService.CreateSpellJsonAsync(userInput);

            // Validate and process JSON
            ProcessSpellJson(spellJson);
        }
        catch (System.Exception e)
        {
            Debug.LogError($"Error creating spell: {e.Message}");
            ShowErrorToUser(e.Message);
        }
    }
}
```

## Rule 2: 3-Layer Validation Strategy

JSON validation generated by the LLM is critical. Use a defensive layered approach.

### Layer 1: API-Level Enforcement (Function Calling)

Use the LLM API's "Function Calling" or "Tool Use" feature to force structured output.

```csharp
private string BuildRequestPayload(string userPrompt)
{
    var payload = new
    {
        model = "gpt-4",
        messages = new[]
        {
            new
            {
                role = "system",
                content = GetSystemPrompt()
            },
            new
            {
                role = "user",
                content = userPrompt
            }
        },
        tools = new[]
        {
            new
            {
                type = "function",
                function = new
                {
                    name = "create_spell",
                    description = "Create a procedural spell definition",
                    parameters = GetJsonSchema() // Complete JSON Schema
                }
            }
        },
        tool_choice = new { type = "function", function = new { name = "create_spell" } }
    };

    return JsonUtility.ToJson(payload);
}
```

